## 7. OOP Requirements in Detail (C++)

### 7.1 Four pillars

**Abstraction.** `Evidence` and `IReportable` expose only what callers need. `HashService` hides OpenSSL.

**Encapsulation.** All data members are `private` or `protected`. Changes go through validated setters. A closed case rejects every mutation inside the `Case` class itself, not only in the API layer.

```cpp
class Case {
private:
    std::string status_;
public:
    const std::string& getStatus() const { return status_; }
    void setStatus(const std::string& s) {
        if (status_ == "CLOSED") throw CaseClosedError();
        status_ = s;
    }
};
```

**Inheritance and Polymorphism** are covered in 7.3 to 7.5.

### 7.2 Classes, objects, constructors and destructors

Every major class must show the full constructor and destructor set, at least on `Case`, `DigitalEvidence`, `RawBuffer` and `Report`:

```cpp
class Case {
public:
    Case();                                          // default
    Case(std::string title, std::string desc);       // parameterized
    Case(std::string title);                         // delegating -> Case(title, "")
    Case(std::initializer_list<std::string> tags);   // initializer-list
    Case(const Case& other);                         // copy: DEEP copy via Evidence::clone()
    Case(Case&& other) noexcept;                     // move
    Case& operator=(const Case& other);              // copy assignment (self-assignment safe)
    Case& operator=(Case&& other) noexcept;          // move assignment
    ~Case();                                         // logs destruction, releases resources
    static int liveCount() { return liveCount_; }    // static member
private:
    static inline std::atomic<int> liveCount_{0};
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Evidence>> evidence_;
};
```

Requirements:

- Use member initializer lists in every constructor.
- Use `this` pointer explicitly in at least one method (for example, fluent setters returning `*this`).
- Use `explicit` on single-argument constructors.
- Each constructor and destructor writes a line to `AuditLogger` so the lifecycle is visible in `GET /api/demo/lifecycle`.
- Destructors of base classes must be `virtual`.
- RAII objects (`ConnectionLease`, `ScopedTimer`, `std::lock_guard`) release resources in their destructors.

### 7.3 Inheritance and its five types

```cpp
// 1. SINGLE
class DigitalEvidence : public Evidence { /* ... */ };

// 2. MULTILEVEL: Person -> User -> Investigator -> CyberInvestigator
class Person { /* name, id, virtual describe() */ };
class User : public Person { /* email, passwordHash, role */ };
class Investigator : public User { /* specialization, workload */ };

// 3. MULTIPLE: two unrelated bases
class ICertifiable {                       // pure interface
public:
    virtual std::vector<std::string> certifications() const = 0;
    virtual ~ICertifiable() = default;
};
class CyberInvestigator : virtual public Investigator, public ICertifiable {
    /* ... */ };

// 4. HIERARCHICAL: one base, many derived
class FieldInvestigator : virtual public Investigator { /* ... */ };
// (Evidence -> Digital/Physical/Testimonial is the second hierarchical example)

// 5. HYBRID: hierarchical + multiple, solved with virtual inheritance (diamond)
class TaskForceLead : public CyberInvestigator, public FieldInvestigator {
public:
    TaskForceLead(...) : Investigator(...), CyberInvestigator(...), FieldInvestigator(...) {}
    // the most-derived class initializes the shared virtual base
};
```

Requirements:

- Each of the five types must be labelled with a comment in code and listed in the viva table.
- Demonstrate the diamond problem and show that virtual inheritance gives `TaskForceLead` exactly one `Investigator` sub-object (print and compare addresses in the demo).
- Demonstrate constructor and destructor order across levels (base first, reverse on destruction).
- Show access specifiers (`public`, `protected`, `private`) in at least one inheritance and member context.

### 7.4 Polymorphism: overloading and overriding

**Compile-time (overloading):**

```cpp
class ReportService {
public:
    std::string generate(const Case& c);                         // case summary
    std::string generate(const Evidence& e);                     // evidence report
    std::string generate(const Investigator& i, int days);       // activity report
    std::string generate(const Case& c, bool includeEvidence);   // summary + evidence
};
```

Also overload constructors and `EvidenceService::addEvidence(...)` for different input shapes (JSON, file path, raw buffer).

**Run-time (overriding):**

```cpp
std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Evidence>> items;
items.push_back(std::make_unique<DigitalEvidence>(...));
items.push_back(std::make_unique<PhysicalEvidence>(...));
items.push_back(std::make_unique<TestimonialEvidence>(...));
for (auto& e : items) e->analyzeEvidence();   // 3 different behaviours, chosen at run time
```

`Person::describe()` is also overridden at every level of the people hierarchy. A base-class pointer calling a non-virtual function must be shown once, to contrast static and dynamic binding.

### 7.5 Virtual functions and abstract classes

```cpp
class Evidence {
protected:
    int id_; std::string description_, collectedBy_, integrityHash_;
    std::chrono::system_clock::time_point dateCollected_;
public:
    Evidence(int id, std::string desc, std::string by);
    virtual ~Evidence();                                        // virtual destructor
    virtual void analyzeEvidence() = 0;                         // pure virtual
    virtual bool verifyIntegrity() const = 0;                   // pure virtual
    virtual std::string generateReport() const = 0;             // pure virtual
    virtual std::unique_ptr<Evidence> clone() const = 0;        // virtual copy constructor idiom
    virtual std::string typeName() const { return "GENERIC"; }  // virtual with default
};

class TestimonialEvidence final : public Evidence {   // `final` stops further derivation
public:
    void analyzeEvidence() override;                  // `override` is mandatory everywhere
    // ...
};
```

Subtype behaviour:

| Class | `analyzeEvidence()` | Specific functions |
|---|---|---|
| `DigitalEvidence` | Metadata extraction | SHA-256 hash verification |
| `PhysicalEvidence` | Simulated laboratory analysis | Chain-of-custody log |
| `TestimonialEvidence` | Credibility scoring | Statement consistency check |

Also required:

- A second abstract class or interface, `IReportable` (`virtual std::string toReport() const = 0;`), implemented by `Case`, `Evidence` and `Report`.
- `EvidenceFactory::create(type, json)` returning `std::unique_ptr<Evidence>`.
- A demo showing that deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor is undefined behaviour, on a small isolated sample class.

### 7.6 Operator overloading

Requirements: use at least one member operator, one friend/non-member operator, one unary and one binary operator. `GET /api/demo/operators` runs each and returns the results as JSON.

| Operator | Class | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `operator==` / `operator!=` | `Evidence` | Two items are equal if hash and id match |
| `operator<=>` (or `operator<`) | `Case` | Order cases by priority and created date for sorting |
| `operator<<` (friend) | `Case`, `Evidence`, `Investigator` | Stream formatted output into logs and reports |
| `operator+=` | `Case` | `case += std::move(evidencePtr);` adds evidence |
| `operator[]` | `Case` | `case[i]` returns the i-th evidence (bounds-checked, throws on error) |
| `operator()` | `EvidenceFilter` (functor) | `filter(evidence)` returns whether it matches a type/date predicate |
| `operator=` | `Case`, `RawBuffer` | Copy and move assignment |
| `operator++` (pre and post) | `Investigator` | Increment workload counter |
| `operator+` | `Duration` or `Workload` helper class | Combine values |

### 7.7 Exception handling

```cpp
class AppException : public std::runtime_error {
    int status_;
public:
    AppException(const std::string& msg, int status) :
        std::runtime_error(msg), status_(status) {}
    int status() const noexcept { return status_; }
};

class DuplicateCaseError : public AppException { /* 409 */ };
class CaseClosedError : public AppException { /* 409 */ };
class InvalidEvidenceError : public AppException { /* 400 */ };
class UnauthorizedAccessError : public AppException { /* 401 / 403 */ };
class InvalidInvestigatorError : public AppException { /* 404 */ };
class IntegrityViolationError : public AppException { /* 422: hash mismatch */ };
class DatabaseError : public AppException { /* 500 */ };
```

Requirements:

- Use `try` / `catch` / `throw`, catch by `const` reference, and order catch blocks from most specific to most general.
- Demonstrate rethrow (`throw;`) and exception translation (`pqxx::sql_error` → `DatabaseError`).
- Demonstrate stack unwinding with RAII: destructors run when an exception passes through, and the log proves it.
- Use `noexcept` on destructors and move operations, and `what()` overrides where relevant.
- One central wrapper maps exceptions to HTTP responses so controllers stay clean:

```cpp
template <typename F>
crow::response safeHandle(F&& fn) {
    try { return fn(); }
    catch (const AppException& e)      { return jsonError(e.status(), e.what()); }
    catch (const pqxx::sql_error& e)   { return jsonError(500, "Database error"); }
    catch (const std::exception& e)    { return jsonError(500, e.what()); }
    catch (...)                        { return jsonError(500, "Unknown error"); }
}
```

### 7.8 Pointers and memory management

`RawBuffer` is a mandatory class that holds evidence bytes and must follow the Rule of Five:

```cpp
class RawBuffer {
    unsigned char* data_{nullptr};
    std::size_t size_{0};
public:
    explicit RawBuffer(std::size_t n) : data_(new unsigned char[n]{}), size_(n) {}
    RawBuffer(const RawBuffer& o);               // deep copy (new[] + memcpy)
    RawBuffer(RawBuffer&& o) noexcept;           // steal pointer, null the source
    RawBuffer& operator=(const RawBuffer& o);
    RawBuffer& operator=(RawBuffer&& o) noexcept;
    ~RawBuffer() { delete[] data_; }             // delete[] matches new[]
    unsigned char* data() noexcept { return data_; }
    std::size_t size() const noexcept { return size_; }
};
```

Requirements:

| Pointer kind | Where used | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `std::unique_ptr<T>` | `Case::evidence_`, `EvidenceFactory` | Exclusive ownership of polymorphic objects |
| `std::shared_ptr<T>` | `Case` shared by `CaseService`, `CaseCache` and investigators | Shared ownership with reference counting |
| `std::weak_ptr<T>` | `Investigator → Case` back-references | Break `shared_ptr` cycles and avoid leaks |
| Raw pointer (non-owning) | Observer parameters, `Evidence*` views | Borrowed, never `delete`d |
| Raw owning pointer (`new[]` / `delete[]`) | `RawBuffer` | Manual memory management, required once |
| Function pointer / `std::function` | Pluggable hash/validation strategy | Pointers to functions |
| Pointer to member (optional) | Sorting by different getters | Pointer to member function |

- Demonstrate pointer arithmetic on `RawBuffer`, `nullptr` checks, and const pointer variants (`const T*`, `T* const`).
- Demonstrate (in an isolated, clearly marked demo) a dangling pointer, a double free and a memory leak, and show the fixed versions. Run the fixed code under AddressSanitizer and Valgrind with zero leaks reported.
- Demonstrate `std::move` and ownership transfer (`unique_ptr` moved from the upload handler into `Case`).
- `GET /api/demo/memory` returns: live object counts, `use_count()` of a `shared_ptr`, and addresses showing the single virtual-base sub-object.

### 7.9 Multithreading, mutex and deadlock

Crow runs with `app.multithreaded()`, so every shared structure must be thread-safe.

**Deadlock demonstration (mandatory):** `POST /api/demo/deadlock?mode=unsafe|safe` transfers a case between two investigators from two threads at the same time.

| Component | Concurrency feature |
|---|---|
| `ConnectionPool` | `std::mutex` + `std::condition_variable` (borrow/return connections, blocks when empty) |
| `AuditLogger` | `std::mutex` + `std::lock_guard` so log lines never interleave |
| `CaseCache` | `std::shared_mutex`: `shared_lock` for readers, `unique_lock` for writers |
| `IntegrityScanner` | Background `std::jthread` (or `std::thread`) that re-verifies hashes periodically, with `std::atomic<bool>` stop flag and clean join in its destructor |
| `POST /api/evidence/verify-all` | `std::async` / `std::future` hashing evidence items in parallel, then joining results |
| `Investigator::workload` | `std::atomic<int>` for lock-free counters |
| Per-investigator mutex | Used by the case-transfer deadlock demo |

```cpp
// UNSAFE: thread 1 locks A then B; thread 2 locks B then A -> circular wait
void transferUnsafe(Investigator& from, Investigator& to) {
    std::unique_lock<std::timed_mutex> l1(from.mtx(), std::defer_lock);
    l1.lock();
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(50ms);              // widen the race window
    if (!to.mtx().try_lock_for(2s))                 // timeout = deadlock DETECTED, server never hangs
        throw DeadlockDetectedError("Circular wait between investigators");
    // ... move case ...
    to.mtx().unlock();
}

// SAFE: acquire both locks atomically (or in a global id order)
void transferSafe(Investigator& from, Investigator& to) {
    std::scoped_lock lock(from.mtx(), to.mtx());    // deadlock-avoidance algorithm
    // ... move case ...
}
```

The response must report which mode ran, how long it took, whether deadlock was detected, and the four Coffman conditions (mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait) with the one that the fix breaks.

Other required demonstrations:

- A race condition (unsynchronized counter) fixed with `std::mutex` or `std::atomic`.
- `lock_guard` vs `unique_lock` vs `scoped_lock` usage, each at least once.
- ThreadSanitizer runs cleanly on the safe code paths.

## 8. Core Modules

### Authentication Module
- Register, login, JWT (jwt-cpp), role-based authorization (`ADMIN`, `INVESTIGATOR`) in a Crow middleware class.
- Passwords hashed (never stored plain).
- `AuthMiddleware` uses Crow's `before_handle` / `after_handle` structure.

### Case Management Module
- Create, update, assign investigators, track lifecycle: `OPEN → UNDER_INVESTIGATION → CLOSED`.
- Closed cases are immutable, enforced in `Case` (`CaseClosedError`).
- Duplicate titles throw `DuplicateCaseError`.

### Evidence Management Module
- Polymorphic evidence via the abstract `Evidence` hierarchy (Section 7.5).
- Workflow: upload → `EvidenceFactory` creates the right subclass → `HashService` computes SHA-256 → hash stored in PostgreSQL → `verifyIntegrity()` recomputes and compares.
- Mismatch throws `IntegrityViolationError`.

### Investigator Module
- Profiles, workload tracking, assignment.
- `CyberInvestigator`, `FieldInvestigator` and `TaskForceLead` are constructed by a factory from the stored `specialization`.

### Reporting Module
- Case summary, evidence report and investigator activity report, produced through the overloaded `ReportService::generate(...)`.

### Concurrency and Diagnostics Module
- `IntegrityScanner`, `AuditLogger`, `ConnectionPool`, `CaseCache` and the demo endpoints from Section 7.9.

